 "notes": [
  "Source PDF is titled 'LIST OF MEMBERS OF THE 3RD NATIONAL ASSEMBLY OF PAKISTAN FROM 1962-1964' and lists members under two section headings: 'EAST BENGAL-78' (constituency codes NE-1..NE-78) and 'WEST PAKISTAN-78' (constituency codes NW-1..NW-78). Unlike the 1st/2nd Constituent Assembly PDFs, this one gives an explicit constituency column for every member, preserved verbatim in raw_constituency and lightly normalised into `seat`.",
  "This was the first National Assembly elected after the 1962 Constitution, chosen indirectly via the Basic Democracies electoral college (~80,000 Basic Democrats), not by direct universal-adult-franchise general election; elected_via is set to 'indirect' for the main list, matching the historical electoral mechanism, and 'by_election' only for the one documented mid-term replacement.",
  "East Bengal section: province recorded as 'East Pakistan' per the section heading. Total transcribed: 78 members, including 3 explicit 'Women's/Womens Constituency' seats (NE-76, NE-77, NE-78), recorded as seat_type 'reserved_women'; all remaining East Bengal seats recorded as 'general'.",
  "West Pakistan section: the PDF's own heading is simply 'WEST PAKISTAN-78' with no sub-province breakdown, because West Pakistan was administered as a single unified province ('One Unit', 1955-1970) at this time. Per the extraction rule to use null when the section heading does not indicate a specific province, province is left null for every West Pakistan entry even though many raw_constituency strings hint at the modern-day region (e.g. 'Lyallpur', 'Peshawar Agency', 'Karachi', 'Quetta-Sibi-Lora Lai-Zhob-Chagai', 'Kalat-Kharan-Mekran').",
  "West Pakistan section: 3 explicit 'Women's Constituency' seats (NW-76, NW-77, NW-78) recorded as seat_type 'reserved_women'; all other West Pakistan seats recorded as 'general'.",
  "West Pakistan entry for constituency 'NW-7 (Bannu)' lists two people: Khan Saifullah Khan, Bar-at-Law, annotated in the source as having 'passed away on 4th April 1964', and Mr. Abdul Sattar Khan, annotated '(by-election)' as his successor for the same seat/address (Vill. & P.O Ghaznikhel, Tehsil Lakki Marwat, Distt. Bannu). Both are recorded as separate member records sharing seat 'NW-7'.",
  "One West Pakistan entry (originally item 72 in the source numbering, 'Mr. Shah Nawaz', address in Tehsil Dipalpur, Distt. Montgomery) has no constituency code printed in the source PDF; seat is null for this record and the gap is flagged in its remarks field. This also means the West Pakistan numbering in the source PDF (1-78) does not perfectly align sequentially with the NW-code sequence — item 72's missing code was the only unreadable/missing case found.",
  "West Bengal-side item originally printed as '66. NE-69 (Noakhali-ii) ... Mr. Razzaqul Haider Chowdhury ...67.' carried a stray, out-of-place '67.' fragment in the source text (an apparent OCR/typesetting artifact); it was dropped as noise since the following entry is correctly numbered 67 (NE-76) in the source.",
  "Total members recorded for Assembly 3: 157 (78 from East Bengal + 79 from West Pakistan, the West Pakistan count including the one documented by-election successor for NW-7 Bannu).",
  "No party column exists in this PDF; party is null throughout, per instructions. OCR text had scattered stray spaces inside words; these were collapsed to normal spelling without otherwise altering names."
 ],
